<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William of Modena</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1184–1251</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> was an Italian churchman and papal legate in the Baltic lands from the 1220s. He mediated between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>, the Livonian Order and the local converts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and helped bring about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stensby">Treaty of Stensby</a> in 1238. In 1243 he divided conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> into four dioceses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Culm</a>, Pomesania, Warmia and Samland. He later became a cardinal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-of-modena.jpg" alt="William of Modena" width="242" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Västgöten at Swedish Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vilhelm_av_Sabinas_sigill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> and is also known as William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabina">Sabina</a>. From 1219 to 1222 he served as Vice-Chancellor of the Holy Roman Church, and in May 1222 he was named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>. Popes Honorius III and Gregory IX appointed him legate many times, and in the end he resigned his see to give himself to these missions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1225 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> to settle the quarrels left by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-crusade">Livonian Crusade</a> among Bishop Albert, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a>, the Teutonic crusaders and the Russians. He won the confidence of all sides and arranged compromises on boundaries, jurisdictions, taxes and coinage, but could not decide who was to be master of Livonia.</p><p>He tried to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> out of the dispute by placing it under papal control with his vice-legate as governor, but the vice-legate later handed it to the Brothers of the Sword. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-chronicle-of-henry">Chronicle of Henry of Livonia</a> was probably written as a report for him; it tells how in 1226, at the stronghold of Tarwanpe, he mediated peace between Germans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>. On 18 March 1234 Gregory IX ordered him, as legate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, to mediate between the Stedinger and Archbishop Gerhard II of Lippe, but the dispute was not settled before the spring campaign against them.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, as papal legate, he passed over the rights of Christian, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Prussia, who was held captive by the pagan Prussians from 1233 to 1239, and appointed another bishop in his absence. In 1236 Gregory IX empowered him to divide Prussia into three dioceses whose bishops were to be Dominicans, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> wished. He held to the plan of several dioceses against a single territory of the knights, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a> allowed him to make the division of 1243.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1243, having resigned his bishopric, William was one of the legates Innocent IV sent to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melfi">Melfi</a> to demand the release of captured prelates and satisfaction for the injuries done to the Church. On 28 May 1244 Innocent IV created him Cardinal-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabina">Sabina</a>. He died on 31 March 1251 and was buried in the church of the Dominicans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William of Modena” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1243 the papal legate <b>William of Modena</b> divided Prussia into the bishoprics of Kulm, Pomesania, Ermland and Samland, each bishop ruling a third of his diocese as a territory; three of the cathedral chapters were incorporated into the Order, while Ermland kept more independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1243 the papal legate <b>William of Modena</b> divided conquered Prussia into four dioceses: Culm, Pomesania, Warmia and Samland.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-culm">Bishopric of Culm</a></li><li><blockquote>The diocese was one of four created in Prussia by <b>William of Modena</b> in 1243, covering the land of the Pomesanians.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-pomesania">Bishopric of Pomesania</a></li><li><blockquote>Founded in 1243 as one of <b>William of Modena's</b> four Prussian dioceses, Samland took shape after the conquest of the Sambians in 1255.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-samland">Bishopric of Samland</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1243 the papal legate <b>William of Modena</b> divided conquered Prussia into four dioceses, one of them Warmia.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-warmia">Prince-Bishopric of Warmia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.25,7.92&amp;year=1227" aria-label="Piedmont on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of William of Modena"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5475 6219h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5475 6219h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.25,7.92&amp;year=1227">What was here in 1227? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
